**To run a one-off go-to-market job from code, such as enrolling 50 contacts into a play, enriching a CSV or backfilling a field, declare it with` defineBatch` or` defineRun` in a file under` outputs/` :` cargo-ai project plan` lists it, and the next` cargo-ai project deploy` runs it exactly once.** A ledger in the project’s deploy state records it before it starts, so no later deploy runs it again.


A resource says what exists. An execution says what to do, once. Declaring it in the repository means the pilot enrollment gets the same review as the play it enrolls into.


## Declare a batch#


typescript


```text
// outputs/2026-10-14-expansion-pilot/pilot.ts
import   { defineBatch }   from   "@cargo-ai/cdk"  ;


import   { contacts }   from   "../../infra/models/contacts"  ;
import   { expansion }   from   "../../infra/plays/expansion"  ;


export   const   pilot   =   defineBatch  (  "2026-10-14-expansion-pilot"  , {
workflow: expansion,
data: {
kind:   "filter"  ,
model: contacts,
filter: {
conjonction:   "and"  ,
groups: [
/* … */
],
},
limit:   50  ,
},
});


```


- **` id`** is what Cargo remembers the execution by: lowercase letters, digits, hyphens and underscores, unique in the project. Start it with a date and executions run in the order you wrote them.
- **` workflow`** is a play, a tool, or a workflow written inline with` defineWorkflow` . For one the project does not define, pass` workflowRef(uuid)` .
- **` data`** is where the records come from:


` kind` Runs on


` filter` a model’s records matching a filter, at most` limit` (required)


` segment` the records in a segment


` recordIds` exactly these record ids


` form` records written in code, checked against the workflow’s form


` file` the rows of a CSV in the repository, at most 50 MB


A play batch takes any kind except` file` , and its model must be the play’s own.


To import a CSV that sits next to the batch:


typescript


```text
// outputs/2026-10-20-import/import.ts
import   { defineBatch }   from   "@cargo-ai/cdk"  ;


import   { enrich }   from   "../../infra/tools/enrich"  ;


export   const   leads   =   defineBatch  (  "2026-10-20-import"  , {
workflow: enrich,
data: {
kind:   "file"  ,
path:   new   URL  (  "./leads.csv"  ,   import  .  meta  .url).pathname,
},
});


```


## Declare a run#


` defineRun` runs a tool or an inline workflow once, on one input:


typescript


```text
import   { defineRun }   from   "@cargo-ai/cdk"  ;


import   { backfillOwners }   from   "../../infra/tools/backfillOwners"  ;


export   const   backfill   =   defineRun  (  "2026-10-15-backfill-owners"  , {
workflow: backfillOwners,
data: { since:   "2026-01-01"   },
});


```


A play runs on records, so it only takes a batch.


## Plan, then deploy#


shellscript


```text
cargo-ai   project   plan       # lists pending executions and the ones already in the ledger
cargo-ai   project   deploy     # applies resources first, then starts each pending execution


```


The plan prints pending executions under “Will execute once, after the resources (id order)” and past ones under “In the ledger, so never run again”. The deploy applies resources first, so an execution can use a play created in the same change, then reports each execution as` started` ,` refused` ,` unconfirmed` or` not started` . Only` not started` (it failed before reaching Cargo, such as a file upload) is retried by the next deploy.` --draft` never runs executions.


Executions are read from` outputs/` , in any folder under it. In a[Manifest](https://github.com/getcargohq/cargo-manifest) repository with resources in` infra/` , that is the` outputs/` at the repository root. A` cargo.json` can name other folders in` executions` .


## Exactly once#


You change What happens


The file name or folder Nothing, the id is the same


An execution that already ran Nothing; the plan lists it as in the ledger


A batch’s CSV, after it ran Nothing; it was read once


The id A new execution, run by the next deploy


Delete one that has not run It never runs


There is no retry. To run something again, give it a new id. A refused execution also stays in the ledger: fix it and give it a new id.


## On a connected repository#


When the repository is[connected to Cargo](https://www.getcargo.ai/blog/deploy-go-to-market-changes-on-merge) , the pull request[previews each execution](https://www.getcargo.ai/blog/preview-go-to-market-changes-before-merge) : records, sends and credits, and an approval gate on large ones. The merge starts an execution only if a plan counted that exact version, the plan was approved, and it now runs on no more records than the preview allowed (up to 10% over a filter’s or segment’s count). Otherwise it is held and stays pending.


## Caveats#


- An execution acts on the outside world. A play it enrolls records into may send emails or LinkedIn actions, and reverting the code does not take them back.
- Executions need the deploy state in your workspace. A project whose` cargo.state.json` still holds the full map refuses them until` cargo-ai project state create` moves it.
- Spelling matters: the filter key is` conjonction` , as in the rest of Cargo’s filter API.


Full reference:[Executions in the Cargo docs](https://docs.getcargo.ai/deploy/executions) . The whole lifecycle:[GTM as code: the complete guide](https://www.getcargo.ai/blog/gtm-as-code-complete-guide) .
